tel 1.0

base geodesy
import soundness

info   angles, positions on the Earth, compass bearings, geohashes and `geo:` URIs as typed values
detail
    _Geodesy_ gives geographic code types for the things it otherwise keeps in bare doubles.
    An `Angle` is a genuine angular quantity — constructed in degrees, radians or turns,
    added and scaled modularly, normalised to a principal or canonical value — so that no
    `Double` ever stands for an angle whose unit is a matter of memory. A `Location` is a
    latitude and a longitude packed into one machine word, from which the initial bearing to
    another location, the great-circle distance between them and the position's geohash are
    computed. A bearing resolves to a point of a 4-, 8- or 16-point compass rose, chosen by a
    type argument, or stays an `Angle` measured from north or east; and a `Geolocation` is a
    location with altitude, uncertainty and parameters, read from and written as an
    RFC 5870 `geo:` URI.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import soundness.angleShowable
    import compassBearings.eightPointCompassBearing
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val london = Location(51.5074.deg, Angle.degrees(-0.1278))
    val paris = Location(48.8566.deg, 2.3522.deg)

    london.bearing[Compass[8]](paris)             // Southeast
    london.surfaceDistance(paris).radians*6371.0  // about 344 km
    london.geohash(6)                             // t"gcpvj0"
    (350.deg + 20.deg).show                       // t"10.0°"
    Compass[16](Angle.degrees(247.5))             // WestSouthwest
    t"geo:51.5,-0.13;u=35".as[Geolocation].uncertainty   // 35.0
    ```

    Two givens are imported by name, since `import soundness.*` brings in no givens. An
    angle's textual form, degrees to one decimal place with a `°`, is `angleShowable`, a
    top-level given because `Angle` lives in a component below the text stack and its
    companion cannot carry it. Which compass a bearing is resolved on is a given from the
    `compassBearings` choice package: `fourPointCompassBearing`, `eightPointCompassBearing`,
    `sixteenPointCompassBearing`, or `fromNorthBearing` and `fromEastBearing` for the bearing
    as an angle. Everything else — the arithmetic on angles, their inspection, the codecs for
    `Location` and `Geolocation` — is in the companions and needs no import.

    The library is two source sets:

    - `geodesy.angle` — `Angle`, its arithmetic, `Degree`, `ArcMinute`, `ArcSecond` and the
      `deg` and `rad` extensions. It depends on nothing but hypotenuse and symbolism, so
      libraries below the text stack (a colour's hue, a rotation) can use an angle without
      the rest.
    - `geodesy.core` — `Location`, `Compass` and the winds, `Directional`, `Locatable`,
      `Geolocation`, `angleShowable` and the `compassBearings` package.

    Both compile for the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native, and everything is exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella, where the `compassBearings` package merges with the umbrella's own.

refer geodesy.Angle
refer geodesy.Location
refer geodesy.Compass
refer geodesy.Geolocation
refer geodesy.compassBearings

entry .Angle   an angle as a quantity: built in degrees, radians or turns, with wrapping arithmetic
  detail
      `Angle` is an opaque type over a `Double` of radians, so an angle cannot be confused
      with a bare number or a length, and its unit is never a matter of convention. One is
      made through its unit — `Angle.degrees(45)`, `45.deg`, `Angle.turns(0.25)`,
      `Angle(math.Pi/4)` or `0.785.rad` — and read back in any: `degrees`, `radians`,
      `turns`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import soundness.angleShowable

      val bearing: Angle = 45.deg
      bearing.show                        // t"45.0°"
      bearing.radians                     // 0.7853981633974483
      (350.deg + 20.deg).show             // t"10.0°"
      (10.deg - 20.deg).show              // t"350.0°"
      (Angle.degrees(100)*4.0).show       // t"40.0°"
      Angle.degrees(375).principal.show   // t"15.0°"
      Angle.degrees(355).canonical.show   // t"-5.0°"
      ```

      Arithmetic wraps as angles do: `+` and `-` between angles, and `*` and `/` by a
      `Double` on either side, all reduce modulo a full turn, so 370° and 10° are the same
      angle after any operation. Two normal forms are available: `principal` in `[0°, 360°)`
      and `canonical` in `[−180°, 180°)`. The literal `45.deg` works for an integer literal
      because it widens to the `Double` the extension takes.

      Rendering is in two places. `inspect` is in the companion, at full precision
      (`7.25°`), so a debug rendering never depends on remembering an import; `show`, which
      rounds to one decimal place for display, is the top-level given `angleShowable` and
      must be imported by name, because the `geodesy.angle` component sits below the text
      stack and cannot put it here.

      `Compass[n](angle)` resolves an angle to a compass point, and `Location#bearing`
      produces one, measured clockwise from north.
  refer geodesy.angleShowable
  refer geodesy.deg
  refer geodesy.Compass

entry .Degree   one degree, as an `Angle`: π/180 radians
    A constant to multiply: `45.0*Degree` is 45°, and `Degree/60.0` is `ArcMinute`. The
    `deg` extension is the same angle times its receiver.

entry .ArcMinute   one minute of arc, a sixtieth of a degree, as an `Angle`

entry .ArcSecond   one second of arc, a sixtieth of an arcminute, as an `Angle`

entry .deg   an angle of this many degrees, as `45.0.deg`
    An extension on `Double`: `51.5074.deg`. An integer literal widens, so `45.deg` works
    too. A negative value is clearest bracketed, `(-0.1278).deg`, or as
    `Angle.degrees(-0.1278)`; `Angle` has no unary negation of its own.

entry .rad   an angle of this many radians, as `math.Pi.rad`
    An extension on `Double`, the same as `Angle(value)`.

entry .angleShowable   renders an angle as degrees to one decimal place with a `°`: `45.0°`
    The `Showable` given for `Angle`. It is a top-level given, so it must be imported by
    name — `import soundness.angleShowable` — because `Angle` is declared in the
    `geodesy.angle` component, below the text stack, and its companion cannot hold a
    `Showable`. The rounding is for display: `Angle.degrees(7.25).show` is `7.3°`, where
    `inspect` gives `7.25°`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import soundness.angleShowable

    Angle.degrees(7.25).show          // t"7.3°"
    Angle.degrees(-30).principal.show // t"330.0°"
    ```

entry .angleInternal   the object which declares the opaque type `Angle` and its extensions
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `Angle` is exported from here to the `geodesy` package, and the
      extensions below are found through the opaque type's implicit scope. Its page under the
      `geodesy` package has the full account.
  entry .Angle   the opaque angle type; see `geodesy.Angle`
    entry .apply   an angle of the given radians
        `Angle(math.Pi)` is a half turn; `.rad` on a `Double` is the same.
    entry .degrees   an angle of the given degrees, as `Angle.degrees(45)`
        The companion's constructor; the extension `degrees` on an angle reads it back.
        `45.deg` and `45.0*Degree` are the same angle.
    entry .turns   an angle of the given fraction of a full turn, as `Angle.turns(0.5)`
    entry .addable   `angle + angle`, reduced modulo a full turn
        `350.deg + 20.deg` is 10°. The `Addable` given, in the companion.
    entry .subtractable   `angle - angle`, reduced modulo a full turn to a non-negative result
        `10.deg - 20.deg` is 350°: the result is the principal value.
    entry .multiplicable   `angle * double`, reduced modulo a full turn
        `Angle.degrees(100)*4.0` is 40°. `ArcMinute*60.0` is a degree.
    entry .divisible   `angle / double`, reduced modulo a full turn
        `Angle.degrees(90)/2.0` is 45°; `Degree/60.0` is `ArcMinute`.
    entry .multiplicable2   `double * angle`: a scalar multiplying an angle from the left
        `2.0*Angle.degrees(90)` is 180°, as `Angle.degrees(90)*2.0` is.
    entry .inspectable   a debug rendering in degrees at full precision: `7.25°`
        Unlike `angleShowable`, which rounds to one decimal place for display, inspection
        writes the `Double`'s own rendering of the degrees, since a rendering that rounded
        would hide the state it exists to show. In the companion, so it needs no import.
  entry .degrees   the angle in degrees, as a `Double`
      `45.deg.degrees` is `45.0`. Not reduced: an angle built as `Angle.degrees(375)` reads
      back as 375 until `principal` or `canonical` is taken.
  entry .radians   the angle in radians, as a `Double`
      The representation itself: `90.deg.radians` is π/2. A great-circle distance from
      `Location#surfaceDistance` is an angle whose radians, times a sphere's radius, are a
      length along its surface.
  entry .turns   the angle as a fraction of a full turn
      `Angle.degrees(90).turns` is `0.25`.
  entry .principal   the equivalent angle in `[0, 2π)`: 375° becomes 15°, −30° becomes 330°
      The form a compass bearing is normalised to before it reaches a `Directional`.
  entry .canonical   the equivalent angle in `[−π, π)`: 355° becomes −5°, 190° becomes −170°
      The form a longitude is stored in, so that a western longitude keeps its sign.


entry .Location   a latitude and a longitude packed into one machine word, with bearings, distances and geohashes
  detail
      `Location` is an opaque type over a `Long`: the latitude and the longitude are each
      stored as a 32-bit fixed-point fraction of a half turn, so a position costs one machine
      word, a collection of them is unboxed, and two equal positions are equal values. It is
      built from two angles, latitude first, and read back as angles:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val sydney = Location(Angle.degrees(-33.8688), 151.2093.deg)
      val melbourne = Location(Angle.degrees(-37.8136), 144.9631.deg)

      sydney.latitude.degrees                         // about -33.8688
      sydney.pair                                     // (latitude, longitude)
      sydney.surfaceDistance(melbourne).radians*6371  // about 713 km
      sydney.geohash(8)                               // t"r3gx2f77"
      sydney.encode                                   // t"-33.868800,151.209300"
      ```

      The fixed-point form holds a coordinate to about a centimetre on the Earth's surface,
      so a coordinate reads back within about a ten-millionth of a degree of what was
      given, not exactly: compare read-back angles with a tolerance. The longitude is stored in its
      canonical range, `[−180°, 180°)`, so a western longitude reads back negative.

      The geographic questions are extension methods on `Location`, found through the opaque
      type's implicit scope:

      - `latitude` and `longitude`, each an `Angle`, and `pair`, the two as a tuple;
      - `surfaceDistance(other)`, the great-circle separation as an `Angle` (by the haversine
        formula), whose radians times a sphere's radius are the distance along its surface,
        so the same code serves the Earth and Mars;
      - `bearing[Compass[8]](other)`, the initial bearing toward another location, resolved
        on whichever rose or angular convention a `Directional` given from
        `compassBearings` provides;
      - `geohash(n)`, the position's base-32 geohash of `n` characters, where a shorter hash
        is always a prefix of a longer one.

      A `Location` encodes as `latitude,longitude` in degrees to six decimal places
      (`.encodable`), the form a `geo:` URI uses, and inspects at full precision as
      `⌖51.4999999718275°,0.12599995365645733°`. With an altitude, an uncertainty or a
      coordinate reference system it becomes a `Geolocation`.
  refer geodesy.Angle
  refer geodesy.Geolocation
  refer geodesy.compassBearings
  refer geodesy.Locatable

entry .Compass   a compass rose of 4, 8 or 16 points, as a type, and the resolution of an angle to one of its points
  detail
      `Compass[4]`, `Compass[8]` and `Compass[16]` are match types naming the winds of each
      rose: `CardinalWind` for four points, `CardinalWind | IntercardinalWind` for eight, and
      those with `HalfWind` for sixteen. Because the number of points is a type argument,
      the type of a resolved bearing says which rose it came from, and a match on a
      four-point bearing is exhaustive over four cases rather than sixteen.

      `Compass[n](angle)` resolves an angle, measured clockwise from north, to the nearest
      point of the rose; each point covers the sector centred on it, so on an eight-point
      rose `Northeast` covers 22.5° to 67.5°. The angle is normalised first, so a negative
      angle is measured anticlockwise and one past a full turn wraps.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Compass[4](Angle.degrees(44))      // North
      Compass[4](Angle.degrees(46))      // East
      Compass[8](Angle.degrees(315))     // Northwest
      Compass[16](Angle.degrees(247.5))  // WestSouthwest
      Compass[8](Angle.degrees(-90))     // West
      ```

      A `Location#bearing` resolves on a rose through a `Directional` given, imported by
      name from `compassBearings`; the four- eight- and sixteen-point givens there call
      `Compass[n]`.

      `CardinalWind` and `IntercardinalWind` show as arrows (`↑`, `↗`); `HalfWind` has no
      `Showable`, so a sixteen-point bearing is shown by matching on it.
  refer geodesy.CardinalWind
  refer geodesy.IntercardinalWind
  refer geodesy.HalfWind
  refer geodesy.compassBearings
  entry .apply   the point of a 4-, 8- or 16-point rose nearest an angle measured clockwise from north
      `Compass[8](Angle.degrees(315))` is `Northwest`. An inline method: the number of points
      is a literal type argument, `4`, `8` or `16`, and the result is typed `Compass[n]`.
  entry .points4   the four cardinal winds, clockwise from north
      An immutable array of `North`, `East`, `South`, `West`: point *i* is centred on *i* × 90°.
  entry .points8   the eight winds, clockwise from north
      The cardinal winds with an intercardinal between each pair: `North`, `Northeast`,
      `East`, … `Northwest`. Point *i* is centred on *i* × 45°.
  entry .points16   the sixteen winds, clockwise from north
      The eight winds with a half wind between each pair: `North`, `NorthNortheast`,
      `Northeast`, … `NorthNorthwest`. Every fourth point is a cardinal wind, and point *i* is
      centred on *i* × 22.5°.

entry .CardinalWind   the four cardinal points: `North`, `East`, `South` and `West`
  detail
      The points of a `Compass[4]` rose, and the cardinal points of every larger one. The
      cases are exported to the `geodesy` package (and the umbrella), so they are written
      bare: `North`, not `CardinalWind.North`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Compass[4](Angle.degrees(100)) match
        case North => t"up"
        case East  => t"right"
        case South => t"down"
        case West  => t"left"
      ```
  refer geodesy.Compass
  entry .showable   renders a cardinal wind as an arrow: `↑`, `→`, `↓` or `←`
      In the companion, so it needs no import: `North.show` is `t"↑"`.

entry .IntercardinalWind   the four intercardinal points: `Northeast`, `Southeast`, `Southwest` and `Northwest`
  detail
      The points between the cardinal winds, which a `Compass[8]` rose adds to
      `CardinalWind`. The cases are exported to the `geodesy` package and the umbrella, so
      they are written bare: `Northeast`.
  refer geodesy.Compass
  entry .showable   renders an intercardinal wind as a diagonal arrow: `↗`, `↘`, `↙` or `↖`
      In the companion, so it needs no import: `Northeast.show` is `t"↗"`.

entry .HalfWind   the eight half winds of a 16-point rose, from `NorthNortheast` to `NorthNorthwest`
    The points between each cardinal and intercardinal wind, which a `Compass[16]` rose adds
    to the eight: `NorthNortheast`, `EastNortheast`, `EastSoutheast`, `SouthSoutheast`,
    `SouthSouthwest`, `WestSouthwest`, `WestNorthwest` and `NorthNorthwest`. The cases are
    exported to the `geodesy` package and the umbrella, so they are written bare. Unlike
    `CardinalWind` and `IntercardinalWind`, a half wind has no `Showable` arrow.

entry .North   the cardinal wind north, 0°: `CardinalWind.North`, exported
entry .East   the cardinal wind east, 90°: `CardinalWind.East`, exported
entry .South   the cardinal wind south, 180°: `CardinalWind.South`, exported
entry .West   the cardinal wind west, 270°: `CardinalWind.West`, exported
entry .Northeast   the intercardinal wind northeast, 45°: `IntercardinalWind.Northeast`, exported
entry .Southeast   the intercardinal wind southeast, 135°: `IntercardinalWind.Southeast`, exported
entry .Southwest   the intercardinal wind southwest, 225°: `IntercardinalWind.Southwest`, exported
entry .Northwest   the intercardinal wind northwest, 315°: `IntercardinalWind.Northwest`, exported
entry .NorthNortheast   the half wind north-northeast, 22.5°: `HalfWind.NorthNortheast`, exported
entry .EastNortheast   the half wind east-northeast, 67.5°: `HalfWind.EastNortheast`, exported
entry .EastSoutheast   the half wind east-southeast, 112.5°: `HalfWind.EastSoutheast`, exported
entry .SouthSoutheast   the half wind south-southeast, 157.5°: `HalfWind.SouthSoutheast`, exported
entry .SouthSouthwest   the half wind south-southwest, 202.5°: `HalfWind.SouthSouthwest`, exported
entry .WestSouthwest   the half wind west-southwest, 247.5°: `HalfWind.WestSouthwest`, exported
entry .WestNorthwest   the half wind west-northwest, 292.5°: `HalfWind.WestNorthwest`, exported
entry .NorthNorthwest   the half wind north-northwest, 337.5°: `HalfWind.NorthNorthwest`, exported

entry .Directional   a typeclass turning a bearing, an angle clockwise from north, into a direction value
  detail
      `Directional` is what `Location#bearing[T]` needs of its result type `T`: a single
      method, `direction`, from the bearing's principal angle (in `[0°, 360°)`, clockwise from
      north) to a `T`. The `compassBearings` package provides the standard instances, each
      imported by name: the 4-, 8- and 16-point roses, and the bearing as an `Angle` from
      north or from east.

      A domain with sectors of its own implements one:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      enum Clock:
        case Twelve, Three, Six, Nine

      given Clock is Directional = angle => Clock.fromOrdinal((0.5 + angle.turns*4).toInt%4)

      Location(51.5.deg, 0.0.deg).bearing[Clock](Location(51.5.deg, 1.0.deg))  // Three
      ```
  refer geodesy.compassBearings
  refer geodesy.Compass
  entry #direction   the direction for a bearing, given as an angle clockwise from north in `[0°, 360°)`

entry .Locatable   a typeclass for a value which has a position on the Earth
  detail
      A `Locatable` type can give a `Location` for any of its values: a city, a station, a
      photograph. Its one method, `location`, extracts it, so code generic over located
      things can ask for `T is Locatable` and read a position from each.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class City(name: Text, centre: Location)

      given City is Locatable = _.centre

      def near[place: Locatable](a: place, b: place): Boolean =
        place.location(a).surfaceDistance(place.location(b)).degrees < 1.0
      ```

      Geodesy itself defines no instances and no operation that takes one, so the
      `Location` extensions still need a `Location`; `Locatable` is the seam for a library
      built on top.
  refer geodesy.Location
  entry #location   the position of a value, as a `Location`

entry .Geolocation   a location with optional altitude, uncertainty, reference system and parameters: an RFC 5870 `geo:` URI
  detail
      A `Geolocation` is the content of an RFC 5870 `geo:` URI as a case class: a `Location`,
      an optional altitude in metres, an optional coordinate reference system (`crs`), an
      optional uncertainty in metres (`u`), and any further parameters. It decodes from
      `geo:` text and encodes back to it:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val vienna = t"geo:48.2010,16.3695,183;crs=wgs84;u=35;name=Vienna".as[Geolocation]
      vienna.altitude      // 183.0
      vienna.crs           // t"wgs84"
      vienna.uncertainty   // 35.0
      vienna.parameters    // Map(t"name" -> t"Vienna")

      Geolocation(Location(51.5.deg, 0.25.deg), 12.5, uncertainty = 3.0).encode
      // t"geo:51.500000,0.250000,12.500000;u=3.000000"
      ```

      Decoding needs a `Tactic[Geolocation.Error]` and raises one whose `Reason` names the
      fault; under a tactic which recovers, a partial `Geolocation` is returned, with the
      parts that did parse. Coordinates and altitude are numbers of the form `-?[0-9]+(.[0-9]+)?`
      (no exponent, no leading `+`). As the RFC orders them, `crs` is recognised only as the
      first parameter and `u` only as the first after it; any other parameter, including a
      `crs` or `u` out of place, is kept in `#parameters`.

      Encoding writes every number to six decimal places, so a decoded and re-encoded URI is
      equal in value but not always in text.
  refer geodesy.Location
  refer geodesy.Geolocation.Error
  entry .decoder   reads a `geo:` URI as a `Geolocation`, raising `Geolocation.Error` if malformed
      The `Decodable in Text` given, in the companion, so `text.as[Geolocation]` needs only a
      `Tactic[Geolocation.Error]` in scope. A text with another scheme raises `BadScheme`,
      and a `geo:` text without two coordinates raises `ExpectedCoordinates`; in both cases
      the recovered value is a `Geolocation` at 0°, 0°.
  entry .encodable   writes a `Geolocation` as a `geo:` URI, numbers to six decimal places
      Writes `geo:`, the location's `latitude,longitude`, then `,altitude`, `;crs=…`, `;u=…`
      and the remaining parameters, each only if present. In the companion, so it needs no
      import.
  entry .Error   raised when text is not a well-formed `geo:` URI
    detail
        Error 420: _the geo URI is not in the correct format because …_, followed by the
        `Reason`'s message. Raised by `Geolocation.decoder`, through the
        `Tactic[Geolocation.Error]` in scope.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        capture[Geolocation.Error](t"geo:1,2,3;u=far".as[Geolocation]).reason
        // Geolocation.Error.Reason.BadUncertainty
        ```

        Each `Reason` is numbered as a sub-code of 420: `MissingEquals` (1), _the parameter
        does not contain an `=`_; `MultipleEquals` (2), _the parameter contains more than one
        `=`_; `BadScheme` (3), _the value does not begin with the `geo:` URI scheme_;
        `ExpectedSemicolon` (4), _a `;` was expected after the altitude value_;
        `UnexpectedSuffix` (5), _a `,` or `;` was expected_, for junk after the longitude or a
        non-numeric altitude; `ExpectedCoordinates` (6), _latitude and longitude coordinates
        were expected_; and `BadUncertainty` (7), _the `uncertainty` parameter was not a
        valid number_.
    entry .Reason   why a `geo:` URI failed to decode, numbered as a sub-code of error 420
      detail
          An enumeration of `Clarification`s: `MissingEquals`, `MultipleEquals`, `BadScheme`,
          `ExpectedSemicolon`, `UnexpectedSuffix`, `ExpectedCoordinates` and
          `BadUncertainty`, numbered 1 to 7 in that order. See `Geolocation.Error` for each
          message.
      entry #number   the reason's sub-code, 1 to 7
    entry .communicable   renders each `Geolocation.Error.Reason` as its message
    entry #reason   why the URI failed to decode, a `Geolocation.Error.Reason`
  entry #location   the latitude and longitude, as a `Location`
  entry #altitude   the altitude in metres, or `Unset`
  entry #crs   the coordinate reference system named by the `crs` parameter, or `Unset` (meaning WGS-84)
  entry #uncertainty   the uncertainty in metres, from the `u` parameter, or `Unset`
  entry #parameters   every parameter after `crs` and `u`, by name, as `Map[Text, Text]`

entry .compassBearings   the conventions a bearing may be resolved on, each a `Directional` given to import by name
  detail
      `Location#bearing[T]` needs a `T is Directional`, and which one is a choice — the
      number of points on the rose, or an angle and its zero — so none is in implicit scope
      and `import soundness.*` brings none in: one is imported by name, since a wildcard
      import does not import givens. The package merges with the umbrella's own
      `compassBearings`.

      The same bearing, from London to Paris, under each:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Four points
        code
            import soundness.*
            import compassBearings.fourPointCompassBearing

            val london = Location(51.5074.deg, Angle.degrees(-0.1278))
            val paris = Location(48.8566.deg, 2.3522.deg)

            london.bearing[Compass[4]](paris)   // South
      frame
        label Eight points
        code
            import soundness.*
            import compassBearings.eightPointCompassBearing

            val london = Location(51.5074.deg, Angle.degrees(-0.1278))
            val paris = Location(48.8566.deg, 2.3522.deg)

            london.bearing[Compass[8]](paris)   // Southeast
      frame
        label Sixteen points
        code
            import soundness.*
            import compassBearings.sixteenPointCompassBearing

            val london = Location(51.5074.deg, Angle.degrees(-0.1278))
            val paris = Location(48.8566.deg, 2.3522.deg)

            london.bearing[Compass[16]](paris)   // SouthSoutheast
      frame
        label An angle from north
        code
            import soundness.*
            import soundness.angleShowable
            import compassBearings.fromNorthBearing

            val london = Location(51.5074.deg, Angle.degrees(-0.1278))
            val paris = Location(48.8566.deg, 2.3522.deg)

            london.bearing[Angle](paris).show   // t"148.1°"
      frame
        label An angle from east
        code
            import soundness.*
            import soundness.angleShowable
            import compassBearings.fromEastBearing

            val london = Location(51.5074.deg, Angle.degrees(-0.1278))
            val paris = Location(48.8566.deg, 2.3522.deg)

            london.bearing[Angle](paris).show   // t"58.1°"
      ##
      ```

      Each rose gives a coarser or finer answer of a different type; the angular givens keep
      the bearing exact. Only one `Angle is Directional` should be in scope at a time, since
      `fromNorthBearing` and `fromEastBearing` give the same type.
  refer geodesy.Directional
  refer geodesy.Compass
  entry .fourPointCompassBearing   resolves a bearing to the nearest cardinal wind, as `Compass[4]`
      `Compass[4] is Directional`, by `Compass[4](angle)`: `North`, `East`, `South` or `West`.
  entry .eightPointCompassBearing   resolves a bearing to the nearest of eight winds, as `Compass[8]`
      `Compass[8] is Directional`, by `Compass[8](angle)`: a `CardinalWind` or an
      `IntercardinalWind`.
  entry .sixteenPointCompassBearing   resolves a bearing to the nearest of sixteen winds, as `Compass[16]`
      `Compass[16] is Directional`, by `Compass[16](angle)`: a `CardinalWind`, an
      `IntercardinalWind` or a `HalfWind`.
  entry .fromNorthBearing   gives a bearing as an `Angle` clockwise from north, in `[0°, 360°)`
      `Angle is Directional`, as the identity: the navigator's convention, where 90° is east.
  entry .fromEastBearing   gives a bearing as an `Angle` from east: the compass bearing less 90°
      `Angle is Directional`, subtracting a quarter turn from the bearing, so due east is 0°
      and due north 270°. Since angle subtraction wraps to a non-negative result, the angle
      is in `[0°, 360°)` and still increases clockwise; it is not the anticlockwise angle of
      mathematical convention.

entry .internal   the object which declares the opaque type `Location` and its extensions
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: `Location` is exported from here to the `geodesy` package, and the
      extensions below are found through the opaque type's implicit scope. Its page under the
      `geodesy` package has the full account.
  entry .Location   the opaque location type; see `geodesy.Location`
    detail
        The opaque type `Location = Long`, with its constructors and codecs. Its page under
        the `geodesy` package has the full account.
    entry .apply   a location from a latitude and a longitude, as two angles or two microdegree integers
        `Location(51.5.deg, Angle.degrees(-0.13))` takes the latitude first. Any angles are
        accepted: the longitude is reduced to `[−180°, 180°)`, and a latitude is not range-checked, so a
        latitude beyond ±90° gives a meaningless position rather than an error.

        `Location(north, east)` with two `Int`s takes millionths of a degree, a compact form for
        tables of coordinates: `Location(51500000, -130000)` is 51.5° N, 0.13° W.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Location(51.5.deg, Angle.degrees(-0.13))
        Location(51500000, -130000)
        ```
    entry .encodable   writes a location as `latitude,longitude` in degrees, to six decimal places
        `Location(51.5.deg, 0.25.deg).encode` is `t"51.500000,0.250000"`: the coordinate pair of
        a `geo:` URI, which `Geolocation`'s encoder reuses. In the companion, so it needs no
        import.
    entry .inspectable   a debug rendering at full precision, marked as a position: `⌖51.5°,0.126°`
        Each coordinate is rendered by `Angle`'s own inspection, at the full precision of the
        stored value, after a `⌖` which marks the pair as a position rather than two loose
        angles. In the companion, so it needs no import.
  entry .latitude   the latitude, as an `Angle` in `[−90°, 90°]`
      Read back from the fixed-point form, so within about a ten-millionth of a degree of
      the angle the location was built from.
  entry .longitude   the longitude, as an `Angle` in `[−180°, 180°)`
      A western longitude is negative:
      `Location(0.deg, Angle.degrees(-74.006)).longitude.degrees` is about `-74.006`.
  entry .pair   the latitude and the longitude, as a tuple of angles
  entry .geohash   the location's base-32 geohash, of the given number of characters
      `Location(57.64911.deg, 10.40744.deg).geohash(11)` is `t"u4pruydqqvj"`, the canonical
      example. A shorter hash is a prefix of a longer one, and nearby locations usually share
      a prefix, though two either side of a cell boundary may share none, so a prefix match is
      a first filter for proximity, not an answer.
  entry .surfaceDistance   the great-circle separation of two locations, as an `Angle`
      By the haversine formula. The angle's radians times a sphere's radius are the distance
      along its surface: `london.surfaceDistance(paris).radians*6371` is about 344 km on the
      Earth. Symmetric, and zero from a location to itself; the poles are half a turn apart.
  entry .bearing   the initial bearing toward another location, resolved by a `Directional` given
      `here.bearing[Compass[8]](there)` takes the great-circle bearing at `here`, clockwise
      from north and normalised to `[0°, 360°)`, and passes it to the `T is Directional` in
      scope: a compass rose or an angular convention from `compassBearings`, imported by
      name. Along a great circle the bearing changes as one travels, so this is the bearing
      at departure.
